# error-code-emission-allowlist (T-2699, T-2698 G1/G2)
#
# Error codes that are DEFINED but not emitted, each with a reason saying why and what
# would change that. Git-tracked on purpose (T-2681): a guard whose reported health
# depends on unversioned local state is a guard whose green is not evidence.
#
# THIS FILE IS A LEDGER OF AN OPEN QUESTION, NOT A SILENCER. Every entry below is a
# published refusal the system cannot currently issue — i.e. a place where the
# documented contract promises more than the code enforces. They are listed here so the
# check stays green on KNOWN gaps while firing immediately on a NEW one; each is also
# annotated at its definition in control.rs so a reader of the taxonomy is told.
#
# Removing a line re-fires that code. Adding one requires a reason — "reserved" alone
# is not a reason, because "reserved" without a stated trigger is how these three came
# to read as ordinary refusals in the protocol's error table for months (T-2698).

# ── unenforced concurrency guard ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Documented in docs/reports/T-005-message-protocol-design.md as "Target cannot accept
# commands (already executing)". No handler rejects a concurrent command — a second
# `command.execute` against a busy session is ACCEPTED. Emitting this would mean
# deciding what "busy" is (a command in flight? a PTY with a foreground job?) and
# whether serialising is preferable to refusing; that is a product decision, not a
# missing line. Until then the documented protection does not exist.
SESSION_BUSY   # no handler refuses a concurrent command; would need a "busy" definition first

# ── unenforced TTL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Documented as "TTL exceeded before delivery". No path rejects an over-TTL message;
# it is delivered late instead. Until T-2699 this value was ALSO being squatted by
# artifact.rs for "artifact not found" (fixed — see ARTIFACT_NOT_FOUND, -32024), which
# is why the collision hid the deadness: the code appeared on the wire, just never
# meaning this.
MESSAGE_EXPIRED   # no TTL enforcement path exists; squatting by artifact.rs fixed in T-2699

# ── built, tested, wired to nothing ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The sharpest of the three. control.rs ships `check_protocol_version()` which builds
# the structured error with {declared, required, method}, and
# `check_protocol_version_rejects_when_declared_is_older` proves the builder works —
# but there are ZERO callers outside control.rs. Version negotiation is defined,
# unit-tested, documented, and never invoked, so the hub cannot refuse an out-of-date
# peer. Wiring it is T-2700 (owner: human) because it begins REJECTING live peers below
# the requirement, and this fleet has hosts on binaries ~1000 commits stale (T-2377).
# A passing unit test on a builder is not evidence the builder is called.
PROTOCOL_VERSION_TOO_OLD   # builder exists + tested, zero callers; wiring is T-2700 (human, wire-behaviour change)
